</figure>

Since the sum of the squares on {AB} and {BC} is medial, while twice the rectangle {AB} by {BC} is rational, therefore the sum of the squares on {AB} and {BC} is incommensurable with twice the rectangle {AB} by {BC}, so that the square on {AC} is also incommensurable with twice the rectangle {AB} by {BC}. [!just X.16]

But twice the rectangle {AB} by {BC} is rational, therefore the square on {AC} is irrational. Therefore {AC} is irrational. Let it be called the *side of a rational plus a medial area.* [!just X.Def.I.4]

<div class="qed">Q.E.D.</div>
